diff --git a/docs/audio-pacing.md b/docs/audio-pacing.md
index 966805d..0303718 100644
--- a/docs/audio-pacing.md
+++ b/docs/audio-pacing.md
@@ -9,3 +9,11 @@ The macOS host also requests 512-frame callbacks (10.67 ms at 48 kHz) to reduce
 Considered alternatives were smaller device blocks, a continuous queue allowance, pacing to a wall clock, and asynchronous unstretched output. The first two preserve the existing audio-clock synchronization and pitch-preserving stretcher; the latter two change synchronization behavior and were not selected.
 
 Validation: the added recovery checks fail against the original header (40/42 virtual-time checks pass) and pass with the fix. The full stretcher/pipe suite passes 99/99 checks, including pitch, continuity, sustained slow production, a long stall, flushing on load and the stretch limit. Alternating 700-frame saved-state SC3 runs obtained 512-frame callbacks and had no dry queue or dropped samples. Aggregate run-time differences were within run-to-run variation; the isolated recovery test establishes the specific pacing fix, not a general FPS increase.
+
+## Single-frame recovery in PAL50 and NTSC60
+
+The 14:09:51 SC3 report exposed a remaining feedback loop in the continuous tempo-derived allowance. A 72 ms frame makes the gap guard reserve about 111 ms of audio (72 ms plus the stretcher's lookahead/floor). The previous allowance shrank below that reserve while tempo was below normal, preventing replenishment and sustaining stretching until the old gap aged out.
+
+The producer now retains the configured stretched allowance while playback recovers, then lowers that allowance toward its normal value at 100 ms per wall-clock second. Only the producer accesses this state, and the threshold stays fixed during each pacing wait. This preserves headroom and avoids the original instantaneous 40 ms queue drain.
+
+New virtual-time cases use both 800 and 960 audio frames per video frame (60/50 Hz), 512-frame device blocks, a single 72 ms frame and otherwise 11 ms CPU work. Previously recovery took 1.675/1.941 seconds; PAL50 added 26 frames over 30 ms and a 54 ms pacing wait. With the fix both recover in 0.864 seconds; PAL50 has seven frames over 30 ms and a maximum 32 ms pacing wait, with no dry audio. The previous build fails three new checks (45/48 simulation checks); the updated full suite passes 105/105. The initial long CPU/host frame itself remains a separate cause to investigate.
